NASA	The National Aeronautics and Space Administration, better known as NASA, was created in 1958 after the Soviet Union launched the satellite Sputnik. The agency took over several existing research groups and began operating on October 1, 1958. Its headquarters are in Washington, D.C.
Apollo 11	On July 20, 1969, the Apollo 11 lunar module Eagle landed on the Moon. Neil Armstrong was the first person to step onto the surface, followed by Buzz Aldrin. Michael Collins stayed in orbit around the Moon in the command module.
Eiffel Tower	The Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World's Fair in Paris. It was designed by the engineering company of Gustave Eiffel and stands about 330 meters tall, including its antennas. It was supposed to stand for only twenty years, but it was kept because it proved useful as a radio tower.
Marie Curie	Marie Curie was born in Warsaw, Poland, and later worked in Paris. She won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1903, shared with her husband Pierre Curie and Henri Becquerel. In 1911 she won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for discovering the elements polonium and radium.
Penicillin	In 1928, the Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming noticed that a mold had killed the bacteria growing around it in a dish in his London laboratory. The mold was Penicillium, and the substance it made became the first widely used antibiotic, penicillin.
Mount Everest	Mount Everest, on the border between Nepal and China, is the highest mountain above sea level at 8,849 meters. Edmund Hillary of New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa, were the first climbers confirmed to reach the summit, on May 29, 1953.
